\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Noise models effect comparison given the same noise magnitude shown for a single silhouette line. Gummeson's~\cite  {gummeson22pose} noise model (a) perturbs the line parameters directly, while our model (b) perturbs the intersection points with the image boundaries. The perturbation is applied in two random directions $\theta _1$ and $\theta _2$ whose magnitude matches the prescribed noise level. Gummeson's~\cite  {gummeson22pose} model tends to preserve direction and just move the line, while ours tends to perturb the corresponding point at infinity that is the main cause of inacurracies in the camera calibration and pose estimation tasks. The image boundaries are shown in solid transparent lines. The dashed line $l$ represents the original silhouette line. The perturbed line $l'$ is shown in solid black. The arrows indicate the perturbation applied to the intersection points with the image boundaries.\relax }}{18}{figure.caption.37}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:noise_model}{{6}{18}{Noise models effect comparison given the same noise magnitude shown for a single silhouette line. Gummeson's~\cite {gummeson22pose} noise model (a) perturbs the line parameters directly, while our model (b) perturbs the intersection points with the image boundaries. The perturbation is applied in two random directions $\theta _1$ and $\theta _2$ whose magnitude matches the prescribed noise level. Gummeson's~\cite {gummeson22pose} model tends to preserve direction and just move the line, while ours tends to perturb the corresponding point at infinity that is the main cause of inacurracies in the camera calibration and pose estimation tasks. The image boundaries are shown in solid transparent lines. The dashed line $l$ represents the original silhouette line. The perturbed line $l'$ is shown in solid black. The arrows indicate the perturbation applied to the intersection points with the image boundaries.\relax }{figure.caption.37}{}}
\newlabel{fig:noise_model@cref}{{[figure][6][]6}{[1][17][]18}}
